{"summary":"HB1527 would require child trafficking prevention and awareness education as part of health education in every grade from kindergarten through grade 12 in public schools. It defines “child trafficking” as a human trafficking offense under the cited statute involving a victim under 18. Local education agencies and public charter schools would have to use state board of education health education standards and provide instruction that is appropriate for students’ ages and grades, with developmentally appropriate instruction and skill building advancing each year. They may use a qualified healthcare professional or social worker to help teach it, if that person meets the cited requirements for helping teach family life.

Starting August 1, 2026, and every August 1 after that, each local school board and public charter school governing body would have to send an implementation plan to the education commissioner and publish it on the school system’s or charter school’s website. The plan must describe how instruction will be delivered at each grade level, the instructor’s professional qualifications, and the materials and resources used. Starting July 1, 2027, and every July 1 after that, they would also have to report to the commissioner, verify that the instruction was provided in the previous school year, and include the same information.

The bill would add child trafficking prevention and awareness education to the list in Tennessee Code Annotated, Section 49-1-1002(a), by replacing specified wording. It would also delete subdivision (a)(13)(B) and the second sentence of subsection (d) of Section 49-6-1304; the bill does not state what those deleted provisions say. The act would take effect upon becoming law but apply to the 2026–2027 school year and later school years."}
